---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
import { pomodoros } from '@/data/pomodoros';
---

<TimerPage
  title="Pomodoro Timer – Free 25/5 Focus Timer, No Login"
  description="Free online Pomodoro timer: 25 minutes of focus, 5 minute breaks, and a 15 minute long break after four. Time left shows in the tab. Works offline."
  path="/pomodoro"
  h1="Pomodoro timer"
  lede="This Pomodoro timer gives you 25 minutes of focus, then a 5 minute break. After four rounds you get a long break, and then it stops."
  config={defaultConfigs.pomodoro}
  faq={faqs['/pomodoro']}
  related={relatedTimers['/pomodoro']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/pomodoro']}
  crumbName="Pomodoro timer"
>
  <Section title="How the Pomodoro technique works" flush>
    <p>
      Francesco Cirillo came up with it in the late 1980s, using a kitchen timer shaped like a
      tomato. You work on one thing for 25 minutes, then take a 5 minute break. After four of those,
      you take 15 to 30 minutes off. The exact numbers matter less than one rule: a pomodoro can't
      be split. If you get interrupted, it doesn't count.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Using this timer">
    <p>
      Press Start and leave the tab open. The tab title shows the minutes left, so you can check it
      from another tab. A rising pair of beeps means focus, and a falling pair means break. The
      cycle goes Focus 1, short break, Focus 2, short break, Focus 3, short break, Focus 4, long
      break, Done. It won't start again on its own.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prefer 50/10? Change Focus and Short break above the Start button. Your choice is saved for
      next time.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Other rhythms">
    <p>Other common rhythms have their own pages, each with a short note on who it suits.</p>
    <ul class="links">
      {pomodoros.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <a href={p.path}>{p.h1}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
